{"id": "21cd9bc8-f992-4ba7-9740-46fad3dc9ffe", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:50:35.958377", "source": "user", "parent_id": "9bd5bfdf-9f66-486f-a8eb-77642f08dfaa", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Proceed to the full design-only deliverable (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility). No execution, no claims of results. Rulings on your open decisions:\\n\\nD1 — Affinity proxy. Use the transparent similarity-transfer proxy as primary: for each target, max Tanimoto (ECFP4) to that target's known-actives subset, with activity-annotated groups taken from the declared corpus. Keep it fully specified (radius, bits, cutoff for \\\"active\\\", tie-breaking, behaviour when a target has <N actives). Add a clearly-labelled secondary/speculative arm using a small learned affinity predictor, but mark it as dependent on externally supplied bioactivity tables and do not let any headline conclusion rest on it. Do NOT invent docking results. Target setting: use a small panel of 2-3 activity-annotated targets of the DRD2/EGFR class so \\\"targeted\\\" means something concrete, plus one target-agnostic property-optimisation condition as a transfer check — the question asks about targeted discovery, so at least one genuine target-conditioned arm is required.\\n\\nD2 — Reference distribution. Declare a versioned ChEMBL-derived SMILES sample as both training corpus and \\\"real-world\\\" reference; version, size, filter/standardisation protocol and train/reference split must be stated explicitly, and the reference used for alignment must not include the training molecules in a way that inflates agreement. Include a GDB-17-style synthesizable-space subset only as an optional secondary reference for the synthesizability-vs-drug-likeness separation; treat it as optional, since it is an additional prerequisite.\\n\\nD3 — Generator provenance. Train-from-scratch small CPU-trainable model as primary, for reproducibility. But because a weak prior inflates the hallucination baseline and can manufacture apparent feedback gains, add a pretrained-checkpoint sensitivity arm as a stated robustness condition (specify which class of checkpoint; if unavailable, record it as a limitation rather than a result). Report the baseline hallucination/validity rate explicitly so the reader can judge headroom.\\n\\nD4 — Hallucination. Confirmed: composite battery (parse/sanitisation/canonicalisability, valence and ring-size sanity, uniqueness, corpus off-distribution rate via fragment/Murcko scaffold log-frequency, plus the contiguous aliphatic-carbon-run artifact detector). Confirmed also: unparseable outputs stay in every denominator, and per-metric exclusion rules must be tabulated so no arm can silently shrink its own denominator. Note that with a from-scratch generator these rates are expected to be high; state that as an interpretation caveat.\\n\\nD5 — Diversity. Confirmed: distributional trio (NN-Tanimoto distance distribution, internal diversity, scaffold/framework entropy plus generic-scaffold count), reported across seeds, at fixed K per arm.\\n\\nD6 — Environment policy: option (b). Declare rdkit + CPU torch + corpus files as named, versioned prerequisites, and add an explicit feasibility subsection that states plainly these are absent in the current container and that nothing was run here. Do not hand-roll p-logP/QED stubs for the primary design; if you want a degraded pure-Python smoke test, present it as a separate optional rehearsal with a caveat that re-implemented descriptors are not comparable to the reference implementations.\\n\\nAdditional requirements for the deliverable:\\n- Compute-matching must be a stated protocol, not a hand-wave: identical decode budget AND identical gradient-update count between A0/A1, with the extra sampling cost of A2's rejection filter logged so a reviewer can see the loop is not winning on raw compute.\\n- Include a weighting-sensitivity element for w1/w2/w3 in the analysis plan; the composite-reward weighting is a researcher choice, so treat it as an assumption and probe whether the headline conclusion survives plausible alternative weightings.\\n- Pre-register decision criteria: state the pattern across arms that would count as \\\"the loop adds something beyond filtering\\\" versus \\\"the loop is equivalent to filtering\\\" versus \\\"the loop degrades diversity/alignment\\\", including what a null or mixed result would look like. Do not predict which obtains.\\n- Feasibility: name every prerequisite artifact and its cost, and be explicit about what is unrunnable in this environment (CPU-only, no cheminformatics stack, no network).\\n\\nReport assumptions as assumptions, keep the two speculative dependencies flagged, and do not fabricate any numbers or reference answers.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "f635c212-f2f0-4671-87de-88be3cff1701", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:54:30.943446", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "21cd9bc8-f992-4ba7-9740-46fad3dc9ffe", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved and is asking for a complete design-only deliverable including all the ruling content. I should produce a comprehensive experimental design document. No execution. What to include:\n\n- Rationale, setup, prerequisites (named, versioned, cost, presence/absence in the container)\n- Arms A0–A4 + pretrained sensitivity arm + target panel + target-agnostic condition\n- Full specification of the similarity transfer proxy: ECFP4 (radius 2, 2048 bits), activity cutoff (ChEMBL standard_reference, pChEMBL ≥ 6 → pIC50/Ki etc. ≥ 6? Definition of \"active\": pChEMBL ≥ 6.5 or activity_value ≤ 30 µM... need to choose and state explicitly), tie-breaking, behavior when target's active count is less than N (fallback: exclude target, or fold in a similarity to corpus-wide drugs? Better: exclude that target from the panel and report), handling of invalid molecules (proxy = 0 or excluded from denominator per D4 tabulation rule).\n- Feedback loop implementation: reward-weighted fine-tuning (e.g., cross-entropy weighted by exp(R/T), i.e., REINFORCE or simple reward-ranked fine-tuning / \"reward-weighted likelihood\") or guided sampling. Pick one as the primary: REINFORCE-style policy gradient fine-tuning with composite R, KL regularization to base? KL regularization is important to prevent diversity collapse — but adding KL confounds the \"is this just filtering?\" comparison... actually KL regularization is part of the loop spec; the A2 rejection control has no fine-tuning. Keep KL as a pre-specified fixed hyperparameter, and note that a sensitivity analysis without KL is optional.\n- Compute-matching protocol: identical decoding budget (N candidate draws) and identical gradient update count (A1 fine-tune = U updates; A0 equivalent: continue training on its own unconditional samples? No — that's what A3 null reward covers. A0 baseline: base checkpoint, no updates. Hmm, \"identical gradient update count between A0/A1\" — the researcher says compute matching must be identical decoding budget and identical gradient update count between A0/A1. A0 has no fine-tuning... to match gradient updates, A0 does U updates of self-training on its own unconditional samples (replay on own samples without reward) — actually that's the natural \"extra fine-tuning steps\" control = my A3 null reward was shuffled reward; A0 vs A1 update-matching means A0 gets U updates of self-training on its own uniform samples. Let's specify: A0 = base checkpoint + U updates of reward-free self-training on uniformly sampled data (likelihood on own samples), identical batch size/optimizer/LR schedule; A1 = identical pipeline but samples are weighted by R. Then A3 = same but reward is replaced with a permuted-noise version of A1's reward distribution (preserving the distribution of weights). This satisfies the \"identical gradient update count\" matching. A1's extra decoding budget: A1 draws M candidates per iteration to select/weight; A0 also decodes M candidates per iteration and uses U of them? To match decoding, A0 also draws the same M candidates per iteration and selects at random. Log the extra sampling cost of A2: A2 must draw N_pool candidates to retain top-K; log the N_pool used; optionally match A2's pool to A1's total draws.\n- Weighting sensitivity: grid of w vectors: e.g., equal weights (1/3 each) as primary; alternatives: (0.5 QED, 0.3 plogP, 0.2 aff), (aff-dominant 0.6), (property-only as A4a is already an ablation), plus a per-target Pareto reweighting. Analysis: rerun headline contrasts on a grid of ~5 weightings; conclusion must be stable across ≥4/5.\n- Pre-registered decision criteria: patterns for \"beyond filtering,\" \"equivalent to filtering,\" \"degradation,\" \"null/mixed.\"\n- Metric tabulation with exclusion rules: table of each metric's denominator (all outputs / valid outputs, etc.). Unparseable ones retained in denominators of validity and property scores (assign minimum property value? For property attainment across all draws: invalid is assigned QED=0? Better: property metrics reported on both full-output (invalid = score 0) and valid-only, with tabulation).\n- Seeds: 5 seeds; CPU feasibility: model size ~10–30M params RNN, vocab ~tens; corpus 100k–200k SMILES; training is days on 224 CPU cores? Roughly feasible.\n- Reference split: train/reference disjoint by scaffold split (e.g., split by Murcko scaffold so that no reference scaffold appears in training? That might be too strict... the researcher said \"the reference used for alignment must not include training molecules in a way that inflates agreement.\" Simplest: disjoint random split by molecule, plus a scaffold-disjoint variant for sensitivity analysis. State: reference = disjoint molecules; scaffold-level splits reported in sensitivity analysis).\n- GDB-17 optional secondary reference.\n- Target panel: 2–3 targets with many actives in ChEMBL, DRD2 (D2 dopamine receptor), EGFR, plus e.g. adenosine A2A or factor Xa... choose DRD2, EGFR, AAA2A/FLT3? Stick with DRD2, EGFR, and one more (say ADRB2 or VDR). Choose DRD2 (CHEMBL216), EGFR (CHEMB203... actually CHEMBL203), ADRA2A? Use well-known ones: DRD2 (CHEMBL216), EGFR (CHEMBL203), and adenosine A2A (CHEMBL257) — these are common in GuacaMol/REINVENT benchmarks. Define active as ChEMBL activities with pChEMBL ≥ 6 (i.e., ≤ ~1 µM), confidence ≥ 6, direct assay format, types B/F; deduplicate molecules; cap at e.g. 2000 actives per target; if active count < 100 after filtering → exclude target and report.\n- Learned predictor secondary arm: gradient-boosted / MPNN on ECFP of ChEMBL bioactivity table, per-target or pooled with target fingerprint; flagged as speculative and dependent on externally supplied activity tables (which are part of the ChEMBL download anyway — but ChEMBL activities are available in the ChEMBL sqlite... if the corpus includes activity tables, the learned predictor becomes less speculative; keep the flag: dependent on a versioned activity-table extract).\n- Feasibility subsection: name prerequisites (rdkit 2024.09.x wheel, torch 2.x CPU wheel, ChEMBL sqlite/SMILES ~ v34, GDB-17 subset optional, target activity lists derivable), state their absence in the container (verified), network disabled, CPU-only; no execution performed.\n- Assumptions and limitations: pretrained checkpoint availability unknown → limitation; GDB optional; smoke-test rehearsal optional with caveats.\n\nAlso include rationale for why this design answers the question, analysis plan (statistical tests: per-seed contrasts, paired bootstrap / permutation tests, effect sizes; multiple-comparison awareness), and a results-reporting skeleton.\n\nLength: thorough but organized. This is the final handoff. No fabricated numbers. Pre-registered criteria stated as thresholds relative to a baseline (e.g., improvements must exceed seed SD and X must exceed the A2 gap in...). Use decision rules with explicit margins defined relative to measured seed variability rather than invented absolute values — some absolute thresholds (e.g., chain-run percentiles vs corpus 99th percentile) can be defined procedurally.\n\nAlso double-check the software prerequisites I might mention: RDKit version — say 2025.09 or \"≥2024.03, pinned\"; I can't verify the current version; express as \"pin at a specific version, e.g., RDKit 2025.03.x (pinned at environment-build time).\" Fine as long as I avoid fabricating false facts; the version naming as a prerequisite is a choice, not a claim about availability. Note that the exact pin is to be fixed at provisioning.\n\nCompute estimates: be honest, clearly labeled as planning estimates (not measured). Model: 2-layer GRU with 512 hidden, vocab ~600 SMILES chars, ~5M params. Corpus 200k molecules. Pretraining: ~2–3 days on 32–64 CPU threads (estimate). Fine-tuning 1000 updates, batch 64, M=256 candidates per batch → decoding cost ~250k sequences per fine-tuning run; fine on CPU. Fine.\n\nStructure the answer into sections. Include a controls table. Include the exclusion-rule tabulation. Include pre-registration. Include weighting sensitivity. Include feasibility. Flag the two speculative dependencies (pretrained checkpoint; learned affinity predictor + GDB optional). \n\nEvidence field: repo investigation + how each ruling maps to design elements. Assumptions separate. Uncertainty: open provisioning items.\n\nAlright, let's write. Keep it exhaustive but concise. Might avoid the literal markdown table due to length, but tables help. Use compact tables.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# DESIGN-ONLY DELIVERABLE — Does an embedded chemical-feedback loop (p-logP + QED + affinity proxy) improve targeted molecule generation beyond filtering, without sacrificing diversity or real-world distribution alignment?\n\nNo experiment has been run. No numbers below are results; every quantity marked [est] is a planning estimate and every threshold is a pre-registered decision rule, not an observation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale and causal logic\n\nThe question conflates three separable claims: (P) feedback improves property attainment; (H) feedback reduces molecular hallucination; (D/A) feedback preserves diversity and aligns output with real drug-like distributions rather than degenerating into artifacts (e.g., ultra-long aliphatic chains). A naive A0-vs-A1 comparison cannot attribute any of these to the *loop*, because A1 typically differs from A0 in sampling compute, gradient updates, and implicit property selection — all of which independently move every metric. The design therefore centers two isolating controls:\n\n- **A2 (rejection filter on the same R)** answers: is A1's gain indistinguishable from post-hoc selection against the same property functions at matched output properties? A2 shares A1's reward, output size, and (as far as possible) property profile but has no change to the generator's learned distribution.\n- **A3 (null-reward loop)** answers: do A1's changes survive when the fine-tuning signal is reward-shaped noise matched in distribution? A3 shares A1's exact compute, update count, and distribution-shift mechanics, minus the information content of R.\n\nThe feedback loop is credited only for effects that survive **both** contrasts. All other arms decompose attribution (components, weightings, model prior strength, targets vs. target-agnostic transfer).\n\n## 2. Declared prerequisites (nothing exists in the current container; see §10)\n\n| ID | Artifact | Spec / pin | Role | Cost [est] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PR1 | RDKit | single pinned release (fixed at provisioning, e.g. a 2024–2025.x pip wheel) | validity, descriptors, ECFP4, Murcko, canonical SMILES | wheel, ~100 MB |\n| PR2 | PyTorch CPU | pinned CPU wheel | training/inference | wheel, ~200 MB |\n| PR3 | ChEMBL extract | versioned (e.g. v34.x) SMILES + standardised activities + pChEMBL/assay-confidence fields; standardisation protocol fixed (salt-strip, neutralisation, single largest organic fragment, RDKit sanitize, duplicate-by-inchikey) | training corpus, reference, activity annotation | sqlite/CSV, ~50–200 MB |\n| PR4 | Corpus split files | fixed random seed; see §4 | corpus hygiene | derived |\n| PR5 | Target panel activity lists | derived from PR3 per §5 | affinity proxy | derived |\n| PR6 (optional, **speculative-dep #1**) | pretrained checkpoint | GCPN/GENTRL-class or a published ChEMBL-pretrained SMILES LM | D3 robustness arm | weights, ~10–500 MB |\n| PR7 (optional, **speculative-dep #2**) | learned affinity predictor + its training tables | small MPNN/GBM on ECFP + target fingerprint, trained on PR3 activities, scaffold-split validation reported | secondary affinity arm | derivable from PR3 but fidelity limited |\n| PR8 (optional) | GDB-17-style subset | e.g. 10⁵ sampled GDB-13/17 molecules | secondary reference for synthesizability-vs-druglikeness separation | file, ~10–50 MB |\n\nPR6/PR7 absence is recorded as a **limitation, never imputed as a result** (§11).\n\n## 3. Generator and feedback loop (primary, from scratch per D3)\n\n- **Gθ**: 2-layer GRU character LM over canonical-SMILES characters, ~5M params [est], vocab from corpus. CPU-trainable: pretraining on 200k molecules, AdamW, batch 256, until val NLL plateaus; 3 pretraining seeds [est 1–3 days/seed on 32–64 of 224 available threads].\n- **Composite reward**: R(m) = clip(w1·s_plogP + w2·QED + w3·s_aff, 0, 1); s_plogP = Gaussian target-window score around a fixed logP target (e.g., window centred on the corpus median, width fixed in advance); invalid m → R = 0, retained in all loop denominators.\n- **Feedback injection (fixed choice)**: reward-weighted self-distillation fine-tuning — draw M candidates per update, gradient on −Σ_i softmax(R_i/T)·log p(cand_i) with fixed temperature T and fixed KL penalty (β fixed a priori) to the base policy. KL is declared *part of the loop*, not a control; the no-KL variant is an exploratory side condition only.\n\n## 4. Corpus, split, and reference hygiene (D2)\n\n- Training corpus: fixed-size sample (e.g., 200k [est]) of standardised ChEMBL drug-like molecules (MW 200–500, heavy atoms ≤ 45 filters fixed in advance).\n- **Reference set**: 50k molecules disjoint from training at the molecule level (same standardisation, same size filter) — used for all alignment metrics so agreement cannot be inflated by memorised molecules.\n- **Scaffold-disjoint sensitivity variant**: recompute alignment with a Murcko-scaffold-disjoint reference (and matching scaffold-disjoint training subset) — reported as a sensitivity panel, because molecule-disjoint but scaffold-overlapping references can flatter a corpus-trained model.\n- PR8 (optional) serves as a second reference to separate \"looks drug-like\" (ChEMBL) from \"looks synthesizable-enumerable\" (GDB).\n\n## 5. Targets and affinity proxy (D1 — primary: similarity transfer)\n\n- **Panel**: 2–3 activity-annotated targets of the DRD2/EGFR class (e.g., DRD2 = CHEMBL216, EGFR = CHEMBL203, adenosine A2A = CHEMBL257; final IDs fixed from the pinned PR3), plus **one target-agnostic property-optimisation condition** (w3 term dropped or replaced by corpus-wide \"drug-likeness similarity\" — pre-declared: w3 dropped) as the transfer check. At least one genuine target-conditioned arm runs the full contrast — required by the question.\n- **Known-actives set A_t**: ChEMBL activities with assay confidence ≥ 6, direct-type assays (B/F), pChEMBL ≥ 6.0; deduplicated molecules; capped at 2,000 largest-potency representatives per target. **If |A_t| < 100 after filtering, target t is excluded from the panel and the exclusion reported.**\n- **s_aff(m; t)** = max Tanimoto(ECFP4(m), ECFP4(a)) over a ∈ A_t. ECFP4 = Morgan radius 2, 2048 bits, RDKit defaults, chirality off, fixed a priori. Invalid m → s_aff undefined → contributes via R=0 rule. Ties: irrelevant to max; for any top-K selection, ties broken by ascending canonical SMILES (deterministic).\n- **Secondary/speculative**: PR7 learned predictor as s_aff; **no headline conclusion loads on it**; if PR7 absent, the similarity-transfer result stands with the stated proxy-validity limitation (\"proxy rewards structural resemblance to known actives, not physical binding\"). **No docking is performed or claimed.**\n\n## 6. Arms\n\n| Arm | Description | What it isolates |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A0** | Base checkpoint + U reward-free self-training updates (identical pipeline to A1; candidates drawn identically but *uniformly* selected, no R) | extra gradient steps / self-training drift / decode budget |\n| **A1** | Full feedback loop (R = w·(p-logP, QED, s_aff)) | the loop |\n| **A2** | A0 generator, N_pool draws, keep top-K by the *same* R; invalid excluded from ranking but counted (§8 table) | feedback ≈ filtering? |\n| **A3** | A1 pipeline with R replaced by per-sample-permuted A1-reward values (identical weight distribution, zero information) | reward information vs. mechanics |\n| **A4a/A4b** | Loop with (w1,w2,0) property-only / (0,0,1)-style affinity-only | component attribution |\n| **A1p** (conditional on PR6) | Pretrained-checkpoint version of A0/A1/A2 triplet | prior-strength robustness (D3) |\n| **A1′** | Target-agnostic condition (w3 = 0, no target) | \"targeted\" vs generic property optimisation |\n| Grid G | A1/A2 re-run under §9 weightings | researcher-choice sensitivity |\n\n## 7. Compute-matching protocol (explicit, per additional requirement)\n\n1. **Decode budget**: every arm draws exactly N = 100,000 candidate molecules [est] per (seed, target) for final evaluation; A1's training draws are logged separately.\n2. **Gradient updates**: A0 and A3 execute the *same* U updates, batch size, optimiser state, LR schedule, and candidate draws M per update as A1; only the selection weights differ (uniform / permuted / R-weighted).\n3. **A2 cost logging**: N_pool required to fill top-K is logged per arm; A2's N_pool is additionally matched to A1's total training draws (with replacement noted if pool exhausted). A reviewer can therefore see A2 gets ≥ A1's sampling compute, and A0/A3 get A1's full update count.\n4. All arms share RNG streams, decoding temperature, and the same fixed output size **K = 10,000** [est] molecules (D5 fixed-K subsampling; deterministic seed).\n\n## 8. Metrics, denominators, and exclusion rules (D4/D5 — no arm may shrink its own denominator)\n\n| Family | Metrics | Denominator policy (fixed) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Property (P) | QED, SA (RDKit sascorer), p-logP window score, composite R, success-rate at pre-set thresholds | reported on **all K outputs**, invalid scored 0; secondary valid-only view shown side-by-side |\n| Hallucination (H) | (i) parse/sanitisation/canonicalisation failure rate; (ii) valence & ring-size (3–8) violations; (iii) duplicate rate post-canonicalisation (uniqueness); (iv) off-distribution rate: log-frequency under corpus fragment (fixed-size circular fragments) and generic-Murcko-scaffold frequency models — molecule scored by mean min-log-prob; (v) artifact detector: max contiguous aliphatic-C run ≥ L*, L* = corpus 99.5th percentile fixed from PR3 before any arm runs | **all K outputs, always**; unparseable outputs retained (they *are* the hallucination signal) |\n| Diversity (D) | ECFP4 NN-Tanimoto distance distribution, internal diversity (mean pairwise Tanimoto complement), generic-scaffold count + Shannon entropy of scaffold distribution | fixed-K samples; distributions reported, never a lone scalar |\n| Alignment (A) | FDD-style descriptor-distribution distance vs reference; Morgan-density and scaffold JS divergence; principal-moments-of-inertia shape plot vs reference; chain-length, heavy-atom, ring-count histograms vs reference tails | fixed-K samples vs fixed reference (§4) |\n\n**Caveat (required, D3/D4):** with a from-scratch generator, baseline invalid/off-distribution rates are expected to be high, giving the loop visible headroom; the A1p sensitivity triplet and explicitly reported baseline rates let the reader judge how much of any \"gain\" is headroom exploitation. If PR6 is unavailable, this remains a limitation, not a result.\n\n## 9. Weighting sensitivity (w1/w2/w3 is a researcher choice — probed, not hidden)\n\nPre-registered grid of 5 weightings: (⅓,⅓,⅓) primary; (.2,.5,.3); (.3,.2,.5); (.5,.3,.2); (.1,.3,.6) [affinity-heavy]. Headline contrast patterns (below) are recomputed per weighting; the headline conclusion is only declared *stable* if its category is unchanged in ≥ 4/5 weightings; otherwise the conclusion is reported as weighting-dependent.\n\n## 10. Pre-registered decision criteria (patterns; no prediction of which obtains)\n\nAll contrasts per (seed, target) across 5 training seeds [est]; seed-level paired bootstrap/permutation tests (p < 0.05, BH-corrected across families) with effect sizes and seed SDs reported. Let Δ(X) denote arm X − baseline on a metric family.\n\n- **\"Loop adds something beyond filtering\"**: Δ(A1−A0) favours A1 on H and (P), **and** Δ(A1−A2) is positive on ≥ 1 alignment metric and on off-distribution rate with CI excluding 0, **and** Δ(A1−A3) favours A1 on H — i.e., gains not reproduced by same-reward filtering or same-mechanics noise — **and** diversity trio for A1 non-inferior to A0 (NN-Tanimoto and scaffold entropy CIs within a pre-set non-inferiority margin of seed SD).\n- **\"Loop ≡ filtering\"**: A2 ≈ A1 on all four families (all CIs crossing 0), with both beating A0/A3. The loop's extra machinery buys nothing over ranking.\n- **\"Loop degrades diversity/alignment\"**: A1 wins (P) and possibly (H) but fails the diversity non-inferiority clause or shows alignment regression (e.g., scaffold entropy collapse, chain-run tail vs corpus) — including any ultra-long-chain artifact spike in A1 but not A2 (signature of reward hacking).\n- **\"Null\"**: A1 ≈ A0 on (P) and (H) across seeds.\n- **\"Mixed\"**: any pattern outside the above four; reported as-is with the failing contrast named. Weighting sensitivity (§9) and A1p (if available) qualify the category, never overwrite it.\n\n## 11. Feasibility (explicit)\n\n- **Verified absent in the current container** (inspection, 2026-10-23): RDKit, numpy/scipy/pandas/torch, any docking/scoring tool, any dataset or checkpoint, pip cache, and *network access itself* (DNS resolution fails; pip download fails). Python 3.14 only, CPU-only (224 cores, no GPU). **Nothing in this design was executed here, and none of it is executable here as-is.**\n- Runnable elsewhere as specced, CPU-only: pretraining a ~5M-param GRU LM on 200k SMILES and all fine-tuning arms are small; all chemistry/descriptor work is RDKit-CPU. Rough planning estimate [est, not measured]: ~1–3 CPU-days pretraining per seed; hours per fine-tuning arm; descriptor/Tanimoto passes over 10⁵×10³ comparisons reducible via folded-bit matrices to hours.\n- Two **flagged speculative dependencies**: PR6 pretrained checkpoint and PR7 learned affinity predictor (plus PR8, optional). Each, if unprovided, is recorded as a limitation; primary conclusions depend only on PR1–PR5.\n- Optional degraded **pure-Python smoke rehearsal** (hand-rolled SMILES checks only) may be offered to validate pipelines/harness plumbing, with the standing caveat that re-implemented p-logP/QED are **not comparable** to RDKit reference values and no science conclusions may come from it.\n\n## 12. Reporting skeleton\nPer (target, arm, seed): full metric distributions (not means alone); headline table = family-level contrasts A1−A0, A1−A2, A1−A3; panels for A4 components, A1′ transfer, weighting grid, scaffold-disjoint reference sensitivity; artifact histograms (chain-run, chain-length vs corpus tail); shape plots; explicit denominator/exclusion table (§8) reproduced in outputs.\n\n## Assumptions vs. fixed requirements\n- Fixed by the task/rulings: design-only; ChEMBL-versioned corpus+reference; similarity-transfer affinity primary; from-scratch primary generator; composite hallucination battery with retained denominators; distributional diversity trio at fixed K; option-(b) prerequisites; no docking claims.\n- Assumptions (changeable, labelled): GRU char-LM as generator class; reward-weighted self-distillation with fixed KL as the loop mechanism; the specific target IDs, pChEMBL ≥ 6 / |A_t| ≥ 100 cutoffs, K/N/U sizes, L* = 99.5th percentile, the 5-point weighting grid, non-inferiority margins in seed-SD units, and decision-rule CI conventions — all are a-priori design choices to be frozen before any run.\n- Open provisioning uncertainties: exact pinned versions; whether PR6/PR7/PR8 will actually be supplied.", "evidence": "Repository/environment observations grounding the feasibility and prerequisite sections: (1) /workspace contains only task.json (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\") — no code, model, data, or reward implementation exists, so PR1–PR8 are declared prerequisites, not references to existing artifacts. (2) Import checks: rdkit, numpy, torch, pandas, scipy all ModuleNotFoundError; pip list shows no cheminformatics/ML/docking packages; no obabel/vina; /opt and /srv absent; no pip cache. (3) Network probe failed at DNS resolution and pip download failed — environment cannot self-provision, so absence of PR6/PR7 is a limitation pathway in the design, not an implicit assumption. (4) 224 CPU cores, no GPU — CPU-only compute-matching protocol and small-model sizing follow from this. Each researcher ruling maps into the design: D1→§5 similarity-transfer spec (ECFP4 r2/2048, pChEMBL≥6, |A_t|≥100 exclusion, deterministic tie-break) + speculative PR7 secondary + target panel incl. target-agnostic arm; D2→§4 molecule-disjoint reference + scaffold-disjoint sensitivity + optional GDB; D3→§3/§6 from-scratch primary with A1p robustness triplet and recorded baseline-rate caveat; D4→§8 composite battery with per-metric denominator table retaining unparseable outputs; D5→§8 distributional trio at fixed K across seeds; D6→§2/§11 option-(b) pinned prerequisites + smoke-test caveat. Compute-matching is §7's four numbered protocol rules; weighting sensitivity is §9; pre-registered outcome patterns (incl. null and mixed) are §10. No results exist because nothing was executed; all quantities marked [est] are planning values.", "assumptions": ["Design-only: the deliverable is a frozen protocol; no arm has been run and no performance quantity in this document is an observation.", "PR1–PR5 (pinned RDKit, CPU PyTorch, versioned ChEMBL extract, derived splits and target activity lists) will be provisioned in some external CPU environment; the design is not constrained to the current bare container.", "Generator class (GRU char-LM), loop mechanism (reward-weighted self-distillation with fixed KL), and all a-priori constan